Ischaemic Colitis
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Sudden left-sided abdominal pain, Urgent bloody diarrhoea, History of cardiovascular disease/hypotension

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-042
Reason for Visit: Sudden, agonizing pain on the left side of the abdomen followed immediately by the passage of a large volume of bloody diarrhoea, causing extreme weakness and near-syncope.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an abrupt onset of severe localized abdominal pain quickly followed by bloody diarrhoea in an older patient with established cardiovascular disease. This presentation is highly suspicious for acute colonic ischaemia or an impending bowel infarction. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to evaluate haemodynamic stability, rule out full-thickness bowel necrosis (peritonitis), and arrange immediate emergency transport prior to the onset of irreversible septic shock.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "A tearing pain ripped through my left side out of nowhere, and then I practically passed out passing a bowl full of bright red blood."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a sudden onset of severe, crampy, left-sided lower abdominal pain commencing 4 hours ago. This was immediately followed by an urgent desire to defecate, resulting in the passage of bright red blood per rectum and bloody diarrhoea within the first hour of pain onset. Denies any recent dietary changes or travel. Patient has a profound history of established cardiovascular disease (coronary artery bypass 5 years ago) and reports a prolonged hypotensive episode yesterday related to taking an extra dose of prescribed antihypertensives by mistake.
Functional Status: Bedbound, pale, and extremely lethargic. Unable to stand due to symptomatic orthostasis and pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of catastrophic bowel infarction and hypovolaemic shock.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 90/55 | HR: 115 | Temp: 37.6 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Patient appears acutely unwell, diaphoretic, with delayed capillary refill and marked conjunctival pallor.
Physical Exam: Abdomen exhibits mild to moderate distension. Palpation reveals intense left lower quadrant tenderness with voluntary guarding along the descending colon and splenic flexure. Crucially, there is no rigid, board-like peritonism to suggest free perforation at this stage. Bowel sounds are diminished. Digital rectal examination confirms fresh, bright red blood on the glove. The presentation definitively matches sudden left-sided abdominal pain, urgent bloody diarrhoea, and a history of cardiovascular disease/hypotension.
Environmental Observations: Soiled garments in the bathroom indicating the urgency and volume of the bloody diarrhoea.
Medication Reconciliation: Took an unprescribed double dose of ramipril yesterday, directly precipitating the suspected hypoperfusion event.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Ischaemic Colitis - Status: Exacerbated (Acute hypoperfusion injury to the watershed colonic areas)
Differentials:

Acute Diverticulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of high-volume, sudden bloody diarrhoea, which is extremely rare in diverticulitis (which typically presents with fever, left-sided pain, and altered bowel habits without massive frank bleeding).

Inflammatory Bowel Disease Flare - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The hyperacute, sudden onset of the pain and bleeding within hours in an older patient with no prior history of chronic insidious diarrhoea or weight loss.

Infectious Colitis (e.g., E. coli O157:H7) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The immediate temporal relationship between the profound hypotensive episode yesterday and the sudden onset of symptoms today, heavily favouring a vascular rather than infectious aetiology.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical risk is critical)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the classic clinical triad for acute ischaemic colitis: sudden severe left-sided abdominal pain, an urgent desire to defecate, and the passage of bloody diarrhoea, all occurring on a background of severe cardiovascular disease and a known recent hypotensive event. The objective findings of hypotension, tachycardia, and diminished bowel sounds point to a significant vascular compromise of the colonic watershed areas (e.g., splenic flexure). An urgent out-of-hours home assessment was mandatory to definitively recognize the shock state, rule out immediate full-thickness perforation, and rapidly coordinate emergency ambulance transfer for aggressive intravenous fluid resuscitation, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and urgent surgical/endoscopic evaluation to salvage the bowel.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Placed the patient strictly nil by mouth (NBM). Contacted emergency services (000) for priority ambulance transport. Placed the patient supine with legs elevated to maximize cerebral and mesenteric perfusion.
Positive Behavioral Change: Remain completely flat on the floor with legs raised while waiting for the ambulance. Keeping the body horizontal physically uses gravity to force the limited remaining blood volume toward the core organs, directly preventing a catastrophic drop in blood pressure and maximizing any residual blood flow to the starving bowel tissue.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance is en route. If the patient loses consciousness, stops breathing, or if the abdomen suddenly becomes hard like a board, the spouse must immediately update the 000 dispatcher to upgrade the priority.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a critical medical emergency requiring hospital admission to restore blood flow to the bowel.
Clinical Course: The bowel tissue has been starved of oxygen due to low blood pressure. The hospital will provide intravenous fluids and antibiotics, and use a camera or CT scan to determine if the bowel can heal or if emergency surgery is required to remove dead tissue.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to stand up or walk to the bathroom. The profound blood and fluid loss makes standing extremely dangerous and will further rob the bowel of oxygen.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A sudden drop in blood pressure yesterday critically starved the left side of the large bowel of oxygen. The bowel wall has essentially suffered a 'heart attack' and the tissue is now actively bleeding and shedding. The severe pain is the bowel muscle cramping due to the lack of oxygen. The hospital must immediately administer massive amounts of intravenous fluids to forcefully push blood back into the damaged tissue and powerful antibiotics to prevent the dying tissue from causing a severe abdominal infection.
Actionable Steps: Strictly avoid taking any further blood pressure medications until specifically instructed by the hospital doctors. Taking any medication that lowers the blood pressure right now will directly worsen the starvation of the bowel and increase the risk of the tissue completely dying and rupturing.

